  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "What can be said when the observed contingency table deviates greatly from the assumption of independence?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "There is fairly strong evidence that the two variables are associated",
    "B": "It has been proved that the two variables are unrelated",
    "C": "The probability must be changed to a value greater than 1",
    "D": "The sample constitutes the entire population"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "A large departure from the counts expected under independence gives fairly strong evidence against independence and in favor of association. The test provides statistical evidence, not absolute logical proof. Therefore, the answer is A.",
